{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1795379800,"nonce":"mCi6Uts/GqwrbR6Z4RFcu+QCLCM=","title":"Python static_hot test still demands native code on the first call","message":"CI on main has failed since 2026-11-07 in all three native test jobs. The failing test is a_constant_bound_loop_is_never_interpreted in crates/zyntax_python/tests/static_hot.rs. It asserts that the first call of a function with a constant-bound loop runs native code.\n\nCommit 4dac9a22 (issue c88912c) changed that policy on purpose. A static-hot function now starts in the interpreter while compilation runs, and its running loop transfers to native code. The Lua test was updated then. The Python test was not.\n\nCause of the failure. The test passed only when the compile worker finished before the first call. In a debug build with workspace features, 5 of 6 runs lost that race. A package-scoped build passed, so the failure did not show locally.\n\nFix. The test now asserts the order: main is interpreted, the loop asks for code, the frame transfers. It runs with ZYNTAX_DISABLE_WARM_UP=1, so no compile worker can change the order. The order was the same on every run on the Mac and on Linux x86.\n\nAlso found. cargo test --workspace stops at the first failing test target, so CI never ran the targets after static_hot. I ran them locally with the CI features: tuple_key_specialization, zyntax_runtime, zyntax_ts, zyntax_typed_ast, zyntax_wasm and zyntax_cli. All 74 targets pass.","files":null}]}